WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.080
With a lineup ranging from timeless genre
staples like Halloween to modern classics

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:09.599
that include Mandy and The Wailing, it's not
exactly a challenge to find a winner among

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:11.480
Shudder's unhinged offerings.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:13.600
The bigger problem is knowing where to start.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:19.520
Given the stigma that's generally accompanied
the vampire genre since a certain sparkle-skinned

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:24.490
seed of night gained pop culture infamy, we'll
go ahead and give you a free pass for having

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:28.640
likely missed Michael O'Shea's relentlessly
bleak The Transfiguration.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:34.291
O'Shea's genre-redefining debut follows a
desperately lonely young New Yorker with a

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.500
seriously misguided affection for vampire
fiction.

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:43.700
Gritty, ponderous, and altogether mystifying,
The Transfiguration is at once a daring, wildly

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:49.140
insightful examination of inner city alienation,
a torturous exploration of post traumatic

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:55.000
stress, and an obsessive exploration of a
fantasy life taken far past the edge of sanity.

00:00:55.660 --> 00:01:00.800
For the record, The Transfiguration is also
a gruesome, bloody exploration of urban decay

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:05.480
that's wholly unafraid to dwell in scorched
shadows and filthy back alleys.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:10.820
Pitch-perfect performances from newcomer Eric
Ruffin and up-and-comer Chloë Levine help

00:01:10.829 --> 00:01:16.020
ground this moody, profoundly unsettling tale
in a heightened state of reality that often

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.180
feels a little too real for comfort.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:23.520
There are horror movies that indulge in the
fleeting pleasures of jump scares and blood

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:27.420
lust, and then there are movies that seek
to expose you to things that will unnerve

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:31.959
you in ways you didn't think possible — the
sorts of things that stay burned into your

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:34.580
brain for months and years after you've seen
them.

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:38.540
Hagazussa is 110% of the latter school.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:44.509
If you've yet to experience Lukas Feigelfeld's
gothic folk nightmare about an isolated woman

00:01:44.509 --> 00:01:49.499
struggling to maintain her sanity and raise
her infant daughter in the wilds of 15th Century

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.380
Europe, now's the time to dive in.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:55.729
Just know that the film isn't tailored to
horror fans who get their kicks from cheap

00:01:55.729 --> 00:01:57.620
scares and buckets of fake blood.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:07.900
Truth be told, there's hardly an ounce of
fake blood to be seen in Hagazussa's 102-minute

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:08.400
runtime.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:13.560
But what it lacks in gore-fueled mayhem, it
more than makes up for in ominous energy and

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:14.500
gothic imagery.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:20.019
Feigelfeld uses a heavy dose of both to lull
you into a trance-like state before bringing

00:02:20.019 --> 00:02:24.629
the hammer down with a shocking finale that's
certain turn the stomach of even the most

00:02:24.629 --> 00:02:25.640
hardcore horror fan.

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:27.040
Consider yourself warned.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:33.120
Anna Biller's pseudo-horror spoof The Love
Witch is the very definition of a breakthrough

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:37.610
film — though it's admittedly not the sort
of classic horror schtick some would expect

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:38.600
in a genre breakout.

00:02:39.489 --> 00:02:43.379
Set in the modern day, The Love Witch tells
the tale of a beautiful young witch named

00:02:43.379 --> 00:02:48.420
Elaine who's determined to use her spells,
magic, and callous gender-centric fantasies

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:50.220
to make a man fall in love with her.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:55.420
The problem is, her spells and magic work,
and they tend to leave a love-addled dead

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:56.300
man in their wake.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:01.470
When the bodies start piling up, locals start
to take notice of Elaine's bewitching ways,

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:04.180
and the young sorceress' life begins to spin
out of control.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:09.439
Biller takes a bracingly original approach
to this deceptively simple witch's tale, pitting

00:03:09.439 --> 00:03:15.270
Elaine's '60s-esque style and persona starkly
against The Love Witch's modern day setting.

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:20.269
The result is a dizzying, often hilarious,
frequently unnerving film that finds Biller

00:03:20.269 --> 00:03:25.320
telling a bold little anti-horror tale with
some refreshingly insightful views on gender

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:25.820
roles.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:31.640
Holiday horror has been a tradition for years,
with filmmakers, studios, and streaming services

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:36.880
using the concept to reign bloody terror over
every holiday from Thanksgiving to April Fools'

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:37.380
Day.

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:43.320
But back in 1974 — a full four years before
Halloween helped make holiday horror legit

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:44.880
— that was not the case.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:49.730
So it's no surprise that not many people really
knew what to do with Bob Clark's menacing

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:53.060
holiday slashterpiece Black Christmas upon
release.

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:58.389
35 years later, people still don't quite seem
to know what to make of Clark's wicked little

00:03:58.389 --> 00:03:59.340
holiday nightmare.

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:04.060
And okay, that may be because Clark's name
is typically associated with a holiday film

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:09.590
he made a decade after Black Christmas — a
yuletide charmer about a boy who really, really

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:13.700
wants Santa to leave him a carbine-action
Red Ryder B.B. gun under the tree.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:18.840
Yes, the guy who directed Black Christmas
also directed the iconic, family-friendly

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:23.060
holiday fable A Christmas Story — though
we'd argue that Black Christmas is the better

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:23.560
movie.

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:25.460
"Isn't Santa naughty?

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:27.840
Oh, ho ho f---."

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:33.340
It's also widely regarded as one the first
full-on slasher flicks — one that finds

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:37.900
a bevy of sorority girls who are being terrorized
by a madman over Christmas break.

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:44.480
From that now-classic slasher setup, Clark
and screenwriter Roy Moore craft a bleak,

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:49.320
breathless, socially-conscious drama overflowing
with the sort of shocks and genre staples

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:53.960
that have been copied over and over in slasher
movies — only Black Christmas is so much

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:54.460
smarter.

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:59.840
If the past few years have taught us anything,
it's that the so called "Hollywood dream factory"

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:03.120
is an absolute nightmare for aspiring young
actresses.

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:07.920
Even still, thousands of would-be starlets
flock to Tinseltown every year in hopes of

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:09.300
becoming the next big thing.

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:11.800
Most of them will fail miserably.

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:16.460
And those who make it may be forced to sacrifice
parts of themselves they cannot get back.

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:21.500
"Dreams require sacrifice… and so do we."

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:27.680
Released in the fall of 2014, Kevin Kölsch
and Dennis Widmyer's harrowing Hollywood drama

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:31.560
Starry Eyes is a film painfully aware of those
facts.

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:37.350
As such, it now plays as a near-prophetic
piece of horror fiction that — even in its

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:41.760
unbridled, brutalistic insanity — somehow
doesn't feel like it's all that far off the

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:42.260
mark.

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:47.540
On the surface, of course, Starry Eyes really
could not be more realistic, as it follows

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:52.630
the talented young Sarah who, on the verge
of finally landing her big break, has the

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:57.080
proverbial rug pulled by a sleazy producer
with ulterior motives.

00:05:57.390 --> 00:06:02.260
Turns out those motives are far more devious
than anyone might've expected — a fact Sarah

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:05.740
learns the hard way when she eventually succumbs
to his advances.

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:11.660
What follows is a ruthless fever dream that's
part body horror nightmare, part occult drama,

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:13.540
and part vicious Hollywood satire.

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:16.940
All of which likely makes Starry Eyes sound
a bit nutty.

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:20.700
Believe us when we say it is — and in all
the best ways.

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:26.720
If Repulsion-style psychodramas are your cup
of tea, Robert Altman's Images is a legit

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:27.320
must see.

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:33.460
Released to little fanfare in 1972, Images
was the middle film in Altman's unofficial

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:39.140
"Gothic Trilogy," sandwiched between two deeply
simpatico female-centric character studies

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:43.500
That Cold Day in the Park and Altman's overlooked
masterwork 3 Women.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:48.220
Like those films, Images puts a disturbed
woman center stage and surrounds her with

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:49.460
Altman's signature style.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:55.210
Unlike its sister films, Images utilizes style
and setup to cast an unflinching sense of

00:06:55.210 --> 00:07:00.380
anxiety over the action, fleshing out the
dark impulses awakening within a tragically

00:07:00.380 --> 00:07:01.480
fractured psyche.

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:05.980
As for the story, we'll simply tell you that
it plays out like a schizophrenic doppelgänger

00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:10.410
nightmare with the woman in question losing
her grip on reality, and finding men from

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:14.220
her past and present blurring in and out of
seemingly dual realities.

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:19.990
If you're anything like us, your life was
never quite the same after experiencing the

00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:24.900
visceral, relentlessly brutal freak show that
is Hooper's The Texas Chain Saw Massacre.

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:31.110
Shot with virtually no budget on gritty 16
mm, featuring no name actors, and possessed

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:36.580
of a near suffocating sense of hyper-reality,
Hooper somehow created a horror classic with

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:41.680
his twisted tale of cannibalistic hillbillies
out to snack on an unwitting group of youngsters.

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:44.720
There are some things in this film you just
can't unsee.

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:57.860
As for the things you simply can't unsee,
well, if you've already experienced The Texas

00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:00.660
Chain Saw Massacre, then you hardly need us
to tell you about them.

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:06.360
If you haven't, we wouldn't dream of spoiling
the grisly horrors that await you within the

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:08.080
movie's sweaty, white-knuckled 83 minutes.

00:08:08.980 --> 00:08:12.760
Over the years, there have been a handful
of legendary actor and director pairings in

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:13.540
the world of cinema.

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:18.280
One of the more unsung pairings is that of
Jeff Nichols and Michael Shannon.

00:08:18.700 --> 00:08:23.320
To date, Shannon has featured prominently
in all five of Nichols' features, and if you

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:27.800
want to see the duo at their absolute best,
look no further than their chilling, apocalyptic

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:29.620
psychodrama Take Shelter.

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:35.140
Part rural family drama and part doom-fueled
psychological thriller, Take Shelter follows

00:08:35.140 --> 00:08:38.500
a farmer plagued by horrific visions of a
coming cataclysm.

00:08:39.110 --> 00:08:44.420
Fearful of what lies ahead, the man's hard-wired
paranoia leads him to build an elaborate shelter

00:08:44.430 --> 00:08:48.690
in his back yard — a course of action that
threatens to tear his family apart before

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:49.280
the end of days can.

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:55.280
Featuring career-best work from Shannon and
a star-making turn from Jessica Chastain,

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:59.420
Take Shelter finds Nichols playing in genre
tropes with the precision of a horror master,

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:03.960
building the madness to fever pitch that'll
leave your heart in your throat and your jaw

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:04.460
on the floor.

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:09.200
You should know up front that the first 30
minutes of Most Beautiful Island will feel

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:13.300
a lot more like a deeply personal indie drama
than a full-blooded horror film.

00:09:13.960 --> 00:09:18.771
Stick with it, because as the action moves
along, this "inspired by true events" tale

00:09:18.771 --> 00:09:23.890
of a doomed American dreamer morphs into a
neo-noir nightmare that will leave you shaken

00:09:23.890 --> 00:09:24.740
to your core.

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:30.420
Written and directed with visceral intensity
by first timer Ana Asensio, who also stars,

00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:35.260
Most Beautiful Island follows the travails
of the troubled, undocumented Luciana who

00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:40.280
— in desperate need of cash — takes a
mysterious job that pays a large sum of money.

00:09:40.520 --> 00:09:41.780
It seems too good to be true.

00:09:42.020 --> 00:09:43.000
And it is.

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:48.260
Asensio wisely makes you wait to see what
the job is, instead using her film to depict

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:51.220
the day to day nightmare that is immigrant
life in America.

00:09:51.590 --> 00:09:56.320
We find out about that job in film's final
20 minutes, and we can say with all confidence

00:09:56.320 --> 00:10:00.580
that you'll never see it coming — and that
you'll spend the pulse-pounding final moments

00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:04.640
of Most Beautiful Island on the verge of a
fear-induced nervous breakdown.

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:06.040
Sounds like fun!

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:13.640
Few horror sub-genres have cast quite as long
a shadow as that of Giallo Horror, a stylized,

00:10:13.640 --> 00:10:19.529
slasher-centric genre that's given us horror
fare like Dario Argento's Suspiria and Sergio

00:10:19.529 --> 00:10:21.600
Martino's All the Colors of the Dark.

00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:26.600
While Shudder rightfully sports one of the
more impressive slates of Giallo flicks, we'd

00:10:26.601 --> 00:10:31.540
urge you to forgo revisiting those until you've
seen the Giallo entry most commonly credited

00:10:31.540 --> 00:10:33.120
with birthing the genre.

00:10:33.460 --> 00:10:39.120
That flick is Mario Bava's stylish, visually
arresting murder mystery Blood and Black Lace.

00:10:39.380 --> 00:10:45.020
Set largely within the confines of a posh
Italian fashion house, this crafty, blood-soaked

00:10:45.030 --> 00:10:51.170
thriller takes a relatively formulaic "whodunnit"
narrative about a killer offing models, and

00:10:51.170 --> 00:10:54.300
spins it into a savage takedown of the world
of high fashion.

00:10:54.590 --> 00:11:00.640
Along the way, Bava employs all the trappings
we've come to regard as Giallo staples — expressive

00:11:00.650 --> 00:11:05.710
photography, bold use of colors and lighting,
and a masked psychotic killer with an affinity

00:11:05.710 --> 00:11:10.970
for leather gloves — and almost unwittingly
transcribes the formula for Giallo horror

00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:11.680
as we know it.

00:11:12.110 --> 00:11:17.480
In doing so, Bava also made a first-rate slasher
flick that influenced every genre gem that

00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:17.980
came after.

00:11:19.220 --> 00:11:23.960
Over the past eight years, writer-director
Mickey Keating has quietly crafted one of

00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:26.960
the more intriguing genre resumes in the movie
biz.

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:30.920
2015 alone saw Keating release two feature
films.

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:36.620
The first was a flawed but fascinating family
drama/sci-fi confection by the name of Pod.

00:11:37.050 --> 00:11:41.580
The second was a brooding, black-and-white
beauty about a young woman's spiraling descent

00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:43.200
into murderous madness.

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:49.460
Titled simply Darling, that film is as close
as Keating has come to a legit horror masterpiece

00:11:49.460 --> 00:11:54.040
in his still young career, and it features
a positively electrifying performance from

00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:56.280
up-and-coming star Lauren Ashley Carter.

00:11:56.480 --> 00:12:01.900
As for the Darling's plot, it first presents
itself as a simple haunted house chiller following

00:12:01.900 --> 00:12:08.040
Carter's Peggy as she signs up to play caretaker
to a gothic-styled brownstone — which naturally

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:12.420
sports all manner of creepy backstories about
hauntings and occult activities.

00:12:12.740 --> 00:12:15.220
There's even a locked room that Peggy is forbidden
from entering.

00:12:15.710 --> 00:12:21.480
From that admittedly cliched setup, Keating
spins a sinister, surprisingly gory, and wonderfully

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:25.640
complex psychological thriller in the vein
of Repulsion and The Shining.

00:12:25.900 --> 00:12:30.710
While Darling doesn't quite reach the iconic
level of those films, when a horror flick

00:12:30.710 --> 00:12:34.900
looks this good and is so lovingly assembled,
it hardly needs to.

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:37.420
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:40.820
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
movies are coming soon.

